## Visible pre-order record

[History]
Ms. ___ is a ___ y/o woman w/ h/o GERD, HTN, hyperlipidemia,  and significant EtOH abuse who presents w/ sudden onset  abdominal pain and n/v.  Patient was in USOH until ~ 5 pm last  night when she began to feel a "burning" sensation in her  epigastric region.  She describes feeling a similiar "burning"  during episodes of GERD, and took several Tums tablets- her pain  was not relieved this time, however-unlike when her symptoms  have been d/t GERD in the past.  She ate a small amount of pasta  mixed w/ hamburger and green beans for dinner at ~ 6 pm but  quickly grew nauseated and stopped eating.  By 8 pm, her  abdominal pain had become constant and "sharp," ___ in  severity, and was now radiating to her sides and and her upper  back.  The severity of her pain was temporarily reduced by  sitting up and/or by leaning forward- but pain never resolved.   In addition, she had 2 episodes of emesis of undigested food-  dinner- and then bilious fluid between ___ pm.  She has been  unable to eat or drink anything since that time d/t nausea and  her pain.  She was unable to sleep last night, and presented to  the ED this AM for w/u of her symptoms.    She recalls having similar, but less severe abdominal pain and  n/v ___ years ago- for which she presented to the hospital.  She  received a complete w/u for gallstone/gallbladder/bile duct  etiology at that time- all tests, procedures showed no  abnormalities.  Her symptoms resolved w/out further  complications, interventions.  She has not experienced similar  symptoms until now.  Of note, she denies any recent h/o fevers, chills, dizziness,  diarrhea, constipation, BRBPR, melena, hematemesis, or dysuria.   She does report increased fatigue, lack of energy, myalgias,  loss of appetite, and depressed mood over the last ___ months,  however.                    Past Medical History: 1. GERD (well-controlled w/ omeprazole/day) 2. Hyperlipidemia  3. HTN (baseline SBP ~ 130's w/ HCTZ TX) 4. EtOH abuse (averages ___ drinks/day w/ ___ binge  episodes/week of 5+ drinks; has been drinking this amount since  she was a teenager w/ ___ months of intermittent sobriety over  the years; has been sober since ___ 5. Headaches (daily for 15+ years, bilateral, on top of head,  "pounding," associated w/ blurry vision, relieved by tylenol)    Social History: ___ Family History: unknown (patient is adopted, doesn't know medical HX of  biological family)

[Physical examination]
General:  in NAD, appears younger than stated age HEENT:  normocephalic, mmm, EOMI, PERRL, no supraclavicular or  submandibular LAD, no thyroid masses palpable CV:  rrr, nl S1 and S2, no m/r/g, no JVD Resp:  CTAB, good BS heard throughout GI:  NT, ND, nl BS, no spider angiomata or caput medusae present GU:  no CVAT, no tenderness w/ palpation of spine  Ext:  no peripheral edema, w/wp, 2+ ___ pulses palpated  bilaterally, no palmar erythema Neuro:  CN II-XII intact, ___ strength throughout UE and ___,  sensation to fine-touch grossly intact, bilateral 2+  BR/biceps/triceps/patellar/ankle reflexes, FTN and  finger-tapping intact, no asterixis present Psych:  A/O x 3, mood depressed, affect appropriate

[Prior resulted imaging]
[Prior imaging 1: CT Abdomen (CT ABD W&W/O C)]
STUDY:
CT of the abdomen and pelvis with contrast; coronal and sagittal
reformatted images were also generated.

FINDINGS:


ABDOMEN:
Visualized portion of the heart and lung bases demonstrate bibasilar
mild dependent atelectasis.

Two sub-centimeter hypodensities in the left lobe of the liver (4; 9, 16) are
too small to characterize.  There is mild intrahepatic biliary dilatation. 
The gallbladder appears distended but there is no evidence of intraluminal
stone.  The wall of the gallbladder is slightly hyperemic but not thickened. 
There is a small amount of pericholecystic fluid and the common bile duct is
dilated up to 10 mm (4; 21 and 501B; 24).  The pancreas enhances homogenously.
The pancreatic duct is mildly dilated in the region of the pancreatic head,
measuring up to 4 to 50 mm in diameter.  There is subtle haze and trace fluid
in the mesenteric fat surrounding the pancreas consistent with inflammatory
changes of ____. The main portal vein and its major branches are
patent and demonstrate no filling defects.  No pseudoaneurysms are seen in the
surrounding vasculature.  There is no free air or free fluid.  

The spleen and adrenal glands are unremarkable. The kidneys enhance and
excrete contrast symmetrically.  The small and large intestine show no
evidence of obstruction or wall thickening.  No lymphadenopathy is detected. 
Scant diverticula are seen in the descending colon.  There is no evidence of
diverticulitis.

PELVIS:
The bladder, uterus, and rectum appear unremarkable.  Scattered
diverticula are seen in the sigmoid colon without evidence of diverticulitis. 
A small amount of pelvic free fluid is seen.

BONES:
Mild degenerative changes are seen in the lumbar spine with grade 1
retrolisthesis of L2 on L3.  No aggressive-appearing lytic or sclerotic
lesions are noted.

[Prior imaging 2: Ultrasound Abdomen (LIVER OR GALLBLADDER US (SINGLE ORGAN))]
ABDOMINAL ULTRASOUND:
The liver is homogeneous in echotexture, except for
presence of two cysts in the left lobe, also seen on CT one day prior, both
subcentimeter in size.  There is mild intrahepatic biliary ductal dilatation,
but the common bile duct is more significantly dilated measuring up to 10 mm. 
The gallbladder is similarly distended to one day prior, with mild gallbladder
wall edema again demonstrated and unchanged since prior CT.  No gallstones are
seen, and no stone is seen in the common bile duct, despite visualization to
the intrapancreatic portion. The pancreatic duct is again prominent, measuring
up to 3 mm.

The main portal vein is patent, with antegrade flow and normal waveforms.  The
spleen measures 9.0 cm, normal in size and echogenicity.

